1
00:00:01,668 --> 00:00:03,253
The interface is pretty simple.

2
00:00:03,462 --> 00:00:07,299
You put your horizontal X-coordinate here,
vertical Y-coordinate here.

3
00:00:07,508 --> 00:00:09,801
When you're happy with those,
press this button.

4
00:00:10,010 --> 00:00:11,345
Got it.

5
00:00:15,516 --> 00:00:19,186
Leonard, you'll never guess
who I just found online. Hey.

6
00:00:21,020 --> 00:00:23,857
-Nice shot.
-Eh, his giant head did most of the work.

7
00:00:24,066 --> 00:00:25,484
[LEONARD CHUCKLES]

8
00:00:25,692 --> 00:00:29,196
Very mature.
You're lucky I'm out of Silly String.

9
00:00:29,863 --> 00:00:33,492
As I was saying, Leonard,
you'll never guess who I just found online.

10
00:00:33,700 --> 00:00:35,994
-Professor Proton.
-You're kidding.

11
00:00:36,203 --> 00:00:38,163
-He's still alive?
-Yes.

12
00:00:38,372 --> 00:00:42,167
-Who's Professor Proton?
-He was the host of this great-- Hey.

13
00:00:42,376 --> 00:00:45,379
Yes. Sorry, tell me about Professor Proton.

14
00:00:45,796 --> 00:00:49,299
Professor Proton hosted my favorite
science show when I was a child.

15
00:00:49,508 --> 00:00:51,009
I never missed an episode.

16
00:00:51,218 --> 00:00:54,263
He demonstrated scientific principles
using everyday objects.

17
00:00:54,471 --> 00:00:55,681
It was pretty cool.

18
00:00:55,889 --> 00:00:59,184
Aw. It's so cute when you use
the word "cool" wrong.

19
00:01:00,185 --> 00:01:02,729
Like when kids say "pasghetti."

20
00:01:03,730 --> 00:01:05,691
Oh, dear Lord. Leonard, look.

21
00:01:05,899 --> 00:01:08,402
He's still available for parties and events.

22
00:01:08,610 --> 00:01:11,572
-We should hire him.
-Hire him to do what?

23
00:01:11,780 --> 00:01:15,409
Whatever we want.
Hang out, do experiments...

24
00:01:15,617 --> 00:01:18,912
...make him take 12 pictures with us
so we can make a calendar.

25
00:01:19,454 --> 00:01:23,417
It would be awesome to hang out with him.
I just used "awesome" wrong, didn't I?

26
00:01:25,084 --> 00:01:27,212
Well, I'm e-mailing him right now.

27
00:01:27,421 --> 00:01:29,965
-Do you remember his old theme song?
-Of course I do.

28
00:01:30,173 --> 00:01:32,426
BOTH [SINGING]:
Grab your goggles, put your lab coat on

29
00:01:32,634 --> 00:01:35,846
Here he comes, Professor Proton

30
00:02:01,747 --> 00:02:04,833
Hey, uh, I just found out I have to be
at the telescope lab all weekend.

31
00:02:05,042 --> 00:02:07,669
Any chance you and Bernadette
could take care of my dog?

32
00:02:07,878 --> 00:02:10,881
-Why don't you put her in a kennel?
-Why don't you put your mother in a home?

33
00:02:11,048 --> 00:02:12,299
The Proton Resurgence

34
00:02:15,093 --> 00:02:17,554
To be honest, she'd do better in the kennel.

35
00:02:19,097 --> 00:02:21,892
-I'll talk to Bernie. I'm sure it's fine.
-Thank you.

36
00:02:22,100 --> 00:02:24,561
It's happening. Leonard, it's happening.

37
00:02:24,770 --> 00:02:26,855
Professor Proton is coming to our house.

38
00:02:27,064 --> 00:02:28,190
You're kidding.

39
00:02:28,398 --> 00:02:31,360
You mean the guy who used to host
that lame kids show?

40
00:02:31,568 --> 00:02:34,571
And you just got yourself uninvited.

41
00:02:35,030 --> 00:02:38,075
See, I told you I'd find a tactful way
to do that.

42
00:02:38,283 --> 00:02:42,079
-How'd you get him to come to your house?
-As Professor Proton always says:

43
00:02:42,287 --> 00:02:46,083
"There is no problem you can't solve
if you use your noggin."

44
00:02:46,291 --> 00:02:49,086
-And he wrote him a check.
-Yeah, that too. Big check.

45
00:02:51,380 --> 00:02:53,924
Uncle Howard.

46
00:02:58,428 --> 00:03:02,057
Cinnamon's here for her sleepover party.

47
00:03:03,642 --> 00:03:06,520
You know if you had a stroke,
she'd eat you, right?

48
00:03:07,186 --> 00:03:10,816
And it would be my pleasure
to be her num-num.

49
00:03:13,318 --> 00:03:15,987
Okay, so, what do I need to know
to take care of her?

50
00:03:16,446 --> 00:03:17,531
It's very simple.

51
00:03:17,739 --> 00:03:19,950
For breakfast, she has an egg white frittata.

52
00:03:20,158 --> 00:03:22,661
Give her a choice of home fries
or an English muffin.

53
00:03:22,869 --> 00:03:25,831
But not both. We're watching our weight.

54
00:03:26,540 --> 00:03:30,836
Uh, for dinner, something simple, a veal chop,
some scampi, whatever you like.

55
00:03:32,379 --> 00:03:33,880
-Classy dog.
-Yes.

56
00:03:34,381 --> 00:03:37,509
Also, don't forget to close the toilet
or she'll drink out of it.

57
00:03:38,260 --> 00:03:40,095
I feel for you. Heh.

58
00:03:40,303 --> 00:03:43,140
I've got a psychotic mommy too.

59
00:03:48,353 --> 00:03:50,564
SHELDON:
Leonard, are you in bed?

60
00:03:52,649 --> 00:03:54,067
Yes.

61
00:03:54,484 --> 00:03:56,069
SHELDON:
Me too.

62
00:03:57,738 --> 00:03:59,406
Great.

63
00:03:59,948 --> 00:04:01,074
SHELDON:
I can't sleep.

64
00:04:01,283 --> 00:04:02,325
[LEONARD SIGHS]

65
00:04:02,534 --> 00:04:05,244
Well, I can, so shut up.

66
00:04:06,580 --> 00:04:09,708
SHELDON: Do you realize that
in less than nine hours, Arthur Jeffries...

67
00:04:09,916 --> 00:04:13,044
...a.k.a. Professor Proton,
will be in our apartment?

68
00:04:13,253 --> 00:04:16,882
You know if you stay up all night,
you're going to be sleepy tomorrow.

69
00:04:17,090 --> 00:04:20,302
And a sleepy Sheldon is a cranky Sheldon.
And a cranky Sheldon...

70
00:04:20,510 --> 00:04:23,972
...is actually no different
than a regular Sheldon. Good night.

71
00:04:28,018 --> 00:04:30,645
SHELDON:
I'm thinking of wearing a tuxedo.

72
00:04:31,772 --> 00:04:34,274
That's not ridiculous. Good night.

73
00:04:37,194 --> 00:04:39,446
SHELDON:
Do you have cufflinks?

74
00:04:40,655 --> 00:04:42,073
-No.
-Just as well.

75
00:04:42,282 --> 00:04:45,160
Where can you rent a tuxedo
at 3:00 in the morning?

76
00:04:45,869 --> 00:04:48,038
Okay. Good night.

77
00:04:50,332 --> 00:04:53,084
SHELDON:
Nice to meet you, Professor Proton.

78
00:04:54,294 --> 00:04:56,421
Nice to meet you, Professor Proton.

79
00:05:01,593 --> 00:05:04,471
Nice to meet you, Professor Proton.

80
00:05:05,138 --> 00:05:06,139
Nice to meet you, Profe--

81
00:05:06,348 --> 00:05:07,849
[SLAP]
SHELDON: Ow!

82
00:05:28,203 --> 00:05:29,663
SHELDON:
Leonard?

83
00:05:31,873 --> 00:05:33,667
What?

84
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:36,002
SHELDON:
I still can't sleep.

85
00:05:38,755 --> 00:05:41,299
She really, uh, tuckered herself out
at the park, huh?

86
00:05:41,508 --> 00:05:43,677
Yeah. You two were so cute
playing together.

87
00:05:43,885 --> 00:05:48,640
It was kind of fun throwing a ball
and not having anyone laugh at me.

88
00:05:49,224 --> 00:05:53,436
And you were sweet not to throw it too far
so she didn't wear out her tiny legs.

89
00:05:53,603 --> 00:05:56,147
Heh, yeah, that's what I was doing.

90
00:05:58,149 --> 00:06:01,611
There were a few moments today
when I felt like we were a little family.

91
00:06:01,820 --> 00:06:03,113
-Really?
-Yeah.

92
00:06:03,321 --> 00:06:06,157
I never thought of myself as a mom,
but when we were out...

93
00:06:06,365 --> 00:06:08,410
...I felt like someday we could do it.

94
00:06:09,077 --> 00:06:10,287
Of course we can.

95
00:06:11,955 --> 00:06:16,668
Especially if our baby's
as calm and quiet as little Cinnam--

96
00:06:16,877 --> 00:06:19,045
Son of a bitch, she's gone!

97
00:06:19,671 --> 00:06:22,799
-Well, where'd she go?
-I don't know. She didn't leave a note.

98
00:06:24,551 --> 00:06:26,761
You were supposed to put her
back in the stroller.

99
00:06:26,970 --> 00:06:28,805
-No, I wasn't. You were.
-No, I wasn't.

100
00:06:29,014 --> 00:06:31,933
-Yes, you were.
-Yeah, well, you throw like a girl.

101
00:06:39,566 --> 00:06:41,400
I'm getting worried.

102
00:06:42,401 --> 00:06:45,030
Relax, Sheldon,
he's only a few minutes late.

103
00:06:45,238 --> 00:06:47,866
Professor Proton was never late
when he was on TV.

104
00:06:48,074 --> 00:06:50,368
You know, every day, 4:00, he was there.

105
00:06:51,536 --> 00:06:55,582
Unless tornadoes were ripping apart
East Texas, then we'd join him in progress.

106
00:06:57,042 --> 00:06:58,960
[CELL PHONE RINGING]

107
00:07:00,295 --> 00:07:01,880
It's him.

108
00:07:04,591 --> 00:07:06,051
Hello?

109
00:07:07,218 --> 00:07:08,427
Well, I see.

110
00:07:09,512 --> 00:07:11,014
Yes.

111
00:07:11,389 --> 00:07:14,893
All right, we can come get you.
Yeah, we'll see you soon.

112
00:07:15,101 --> 00:07:16,853
-Bye.
-Where is he?

113
00:07:17,062 --> 00:07:21,483
The third floor landing. The poor old guy's
been walking up the stairs for half an hour.

114
00:07:25,779 --> 00:07:26,821
It's really you.

115
00:07:32,202 --> 00:07:34,037
Mr. Jeffries, I am so sorry.

116
00:07:34,245 --> 00:07:36,623
We should have told you
about the broken elevator.

117
00:07:36,831 --> 00:07:38,667
I agree.

118
00:07:40,001 --> 00:07:43,462
Professor Proton,
it's an honor to meet you.

119
00:07:43,672 --> 00:07:45,465
Uh, just call me Arthur.

120
00:07:45,966 --> 00:07:47,509
Leonard...

121
00:07:49,552 --> 00:07:50,595
...did you hear that?

122
00:07:50,804 --> 00:07:53,556
Professor Proton said
I should call him Arthur.

123
00:07:53,765 --> 00:07:55,850
That means we're friends.

124
00:07:56,559 --> 00:08:01,314
No, a friend would have told me
about the elevator.

125
00:08:04,150 --> 00:08:05,610
Look at me.

126
00:08:08,655 --> 00:08:13,076
I can get as close to you as I want without
my mom saying it's going to ruin my eyes.

127
00:08:16,162 --> 00:08:18,665
Is, uh, he dangerous?

128
00:08:20,333 --> 00:08:22,711
Actually, he's a genius.

129
00:08:23,003 --> 00:08:24,754
I am.

130
00:08:25,839 --> 00:08:29,009
That doesn't answer my question.

131
00:08:30,010 --> 00:08:33,430
Mr. Jeffries, I'm Leonard.
This is my girlfriend, Penny.

132
00:08:33,638 --> 00:08:36,391
-Hi.
-Hello.

133
00:08:39,060 --> 00:08:44,024
Well, I hope I haven't, uh, kept the kids waiting
too long for the show.

134
00:08:44,232 --> 00:08:47,360
Oh, no, there are no kids.
No, the show's for me.

135
00:08:48,987 --> 00:08:51,531
Come on, I'll race you...

136
00:08:51,823 --> 00:08:53,283
...Arthur.

137
00:08:57,454 --> 00:09:00,832
ARTHUR:
Is the blond girl really your girlfriend?

138
00:09:01,041 --> 00:09:04,210
LEONARD: Yes, sir.
-You're the genius.

139
00:09:19,225 --> 00:09:21,978
So do you do a lot of appearances
like this?

140
00:09:22,228 --> 00:09:27,108
It's hard to say.
I'm still trying to figure out...

141
00:09:27,317 --> 00:09:29,819
...what this is.

142
00:09:31,946 --> 00:09:35,825
We just wanted to hang out with you
and maybe learn a little about your life.

143
00:09:36,034 --> 00:09:38,912
Well, there really isn't too much to tell.

144
00:09:39,120 --> 00:09:41,456
After the TV show was canceled...

145
00:09:41,664 --> 00:09:46,544
...nobody in the scientific world
would take me seriously...

146
00:09:47,003 --> 00:09:53,468
...so I was forced to do these
children's parties to make a living.

147
00:09:53,885 --> 00:09:58,139
That's too bad. But still, working with kids,
it must be rewarding.

148
00:09:58,515 --> 00:10:01,017
You get bit a lot.

149
00:10:02,644 --> 00:10:05,146
Let me see if I have this straight.

150
00:10:05,897 --> 00:10:08,525
You two are physicists...

151
00:10:09,859 --> 00:10:16,199
...and you want me to do
a children's science show?

152
00:10:17,408 --> 00:10:18,451
Yes.

153
00:10:19,410 --> 00:10:23,622
And if there's time, take 12 pictures with us
in seasonal clothing.

154
00:10:30,505 --> 00:10:32,882
You know, I'm a real scientist.

155
00:10:33,091 --> 00:10:36,427
I have a PhD from Cornell University.

156
00:10:36,636 --> 00:10:38,847
Yeah, that's great.
Did you bring your puppet?

157
00:10:40,098 --> 00:10:42,267
No. No.

158
00:10:43,476 --> 00:10:46,437
I hate that puppet.

159
00:10:46,938 --> 00:10:48,731
Oh, no.

160
00:10:48,940 --> 00:10:54,154
How could anybody hate
Gino the Neutrino?

161
00:10:54,571 --> 00:10:55,864
It's nice, huh?

162
00:10:56,072 --> 00:10:57,740
I got him for 20 bucks on eBay.

163
00:10:57,949 --> 00:11:00,243
Including the shipping.

164
00:11:03,413 --> 00:11:06,291
I'm awake, right?

165
00:11:09,419 --> 00:11:12,130
This is happening?

166
00:11:14,507 --> 00:11:17,302
-Cinnamon!
-Cinnamon!

167
00:11:17,676 --> 00:11:21,264
You know, maybe she doesn't recognize
her name because of Raj's accent.

168
00:11:22,140 --> 00:11:23,183
Good thinking.

169
00:11:23,391 --> 00:11:26,519
[MIMICS RAJESH]
Cinnamon, come to Daddy.

170
00:11:28,021 --> 00:11:31,524
[MIMICS RAJESH]
Cinnamon, where are you, my little lamb chop?

171
00:11:32,901 --> 00:11:35,278
-Nice. Heh.
-Thanks.

172
00:11:35,486 --> 00:11:37,655
When this all blows over,
remember that voice.

173
00:11:37,906 --> 00:11:40,158
It's kind of a turn-on. Heh.

174
00:11:41,534 --> 00:11:43,661
It turns you on when I sound like Raj?

175
00:11:46,998 --> 00:11:49,542
[IN NORMAL VOICE]
Cinnamon!

176
00:11:51,252 --> 00:11:55,465
Okay, as I put the egg on top...

177
00:11:55,673 --> 00:11:57,508
...and the flame goes out...

178
00:11:58,009 --> 00:12:01,888
...and the air pressure decreases
in the flask...

179
00:12:03,389 --> 00:12:05,225
...what do you think will happen?

180
00:12:05,433 --> 00:12:08,144
-I think I know.
-It's gonna get sucked in.

181
00:12:09,437 --> 00:12:11,814
It's going to get sucked in.

182
00:12:12,148 --> 00:12:14,400
Okay, I didn't know.

183
00:12:18,321 --> 00:12:19,697
Yes.

184
00:12:21,616 --> 00:12:23,660
See, I'm not a scientist like them. Heh.

185
00:12:24,702 --> 00:12:26,996
I figured that out.

186
00:12:28,331 --> 00:12:31,459
-Potato clock. Do potato clock.
-What's that?

187
00:12:31,668 --> 00:12:35,129
I power a clock with a potato.

188
00:12:35,338 --> 00:12:38,341
Shut up. You can do that?

189
00:12:41,010 --> 00:12:44,847
I mean, heh, wouldn't that solve
the world's energy crisis?

190
00:12:47,684 --> 00:12:49,560
No.

191
00:12:51,521 --> 00:12:56,150
Look, guys, keep your money.
I think I'm done.

192
00:12:56,359 --> 00:13:01,197
What's wrong? Is she upsetting you?
Because I can make her go away.

193
00:13:02,365 --> 00:13:06,202
No, she's the only reason
I've stayed this long.

194
00:13:08,329 --> 00:13:09,539
Then what is it?

195
00:13:10,039 --> 00:13:12,625
I don't know. I think I'm just--

196
00:13:12,875 --> 00:13:17,880
I just don't want to be
Professor Proton anymore.

197
00:13:18,589 --> 00:13:22,719
Well, how can you say that?
Professor Proton's the best.

198
00:13:23,469 --> 00:13:25,638
What has it ever gotten me?

199
00:13:25,847 --> 00:13:32,145
I mean, I'm an 83-year-old man
who has potatoes in his suitcase.

200
00:13:32,979 --> 00:13:35,565
Other scientists think I'm a joke.

201
00:13:36,190 --> 00:13:41,404
And the puppeteer who did Gino...

202
00:13:42,488 --> 00:13:44,574
...well, he also...

203
00:13:46,284 --> 00:13:48,286
...did my wife.

204
00:13:53,124 --> 00:13:56,544
Mr. Jeffries, I need to show you something.

205
00:13:59,797 --> 00:14:02,841
-I'm sorry to hear about your troubles.
-Uh, thanks.

206
00:14:03,051 --> 00:14:07,764
But if you don't mind me asking, uh,
the potato clock, how does it work?

207
00:14:09,098 --> 00:14:12,643
Is it a trick clock or a trick potato?

208
00:14:17,648 --> 00:14:20,485
What do you two talk about?

209
00:14:26,824 --> 00:14:30,328
I wrote a fan letter to you
when I was a child in Texas...

210
00:14:30,703 --> 00:14:35,625
...and you sent this autographed picture
back to me.

211
00:14:35,833 --> 00:14:37,335
Do you remember that?

212
00:14:38,336 --> 00:14:43,591
I'll give you a hint. I have a bracelet
with my own address on it.

213
00:14:46,135 --> 00:14:49,347
Well, anyway, um,
you may find this hard to believe...

214
00:14:49,597 --> 00:14:52,517
...but I didn't have any friends growing up.

215
00:14:54,018 --> 00:14:55,812
No, I get that.

216
00:14:58,523 --> 00:15:01,818
But, um, I did have you.

217
00:15:02,026 --> 00:15:06,447
And every day at 4:00,
you'd come to my house on Channel 68...

218
00:15:06,656 --> 00:15:08,741
...and we'd do science together.

219
00:15:09,117 --> 00:15:13,079
If it hadn't been for you, well, who knows
what would have become of me, you know?

220
00:15:13,287 --> 00:15:18,626
Instead of a world-class physicist,
I could've wound up as a hobo...

221
00:15:19,252 --> 00:15:21,504
...or a surgeon.

222
00:15:22,713 --> 00:15:25,633
I bet there are important discoveries
being made every day...

223
00:15:25,842 --> 00:15:29,637
...because you inspired millions of kids
to pursue science.

224
00:15:29,846 --> 00:15:32,181
In a way, their discoveries
are your discoveries.

225
00:15:32,390 --> 00:15:37,395
It's true. A generation of young scientists
are standing on your shoulders.

226
00:15:38,729 --> 00:15:43,526
Well, thank you, guys. That means a lot.

227
00:15:44,068 --> 00:15:47,196
Well, it's important you know
how much you mean to us.

228
00:15:47,655 --> 00:15:50,700
-Uh-oh.
-Arthur, are you okay?

229
00:15:51,075 --> 00:15:53,369
I'm having...

230
00:15:54,078 --> 00:15:56,289
...a problem with my pacemaker.

231
00:15:56,497 --> 00:15:57,832
I'll call for help.

232
00:15:58,249 --> 00:16:00,959
Any chance we could plug it in
to the potato?

233
00:16:05,923 --> 00:16:07,341
No.

234
00:16:14,307 --> 00:16:16,184
[CELL PHONE RINGS]

235
00:16:19,187 --> 00:16:20,521
Hello?

236
00:16:21,689 --> 00:16:25,359
What do you mean you found my dog?
She's with my friends.

237
00:16:26,611 --> 00:16:28,362
Is she okay?

238
00:16:28,905 --> 00:16:31,949
Thank you. Uh, just text me your address,
I'm on my way.

239
00:16:32,533 --> 00:16:36,204
If she's hungry, go ahead and feed her
but do not give her anything starchy.

240
00:16:36,412 --> 00:16:38,539
She's having risotto for dinner.

241
00:16:41,417 --> 00:16:44,879
Your vitals are stable, but let's take you in
for tests just to be safe.

242
00:16:45,087 --> 00:16:48,257
-Want one of us to go in the ambulance?
-I'll do it.

243
00:16:49,425 --> 00:16:51,594
He's not a relative, he's not allowed, right?

244
00:16:51,802 --> 00:16:55,223
-No, it's not a rule. He can go.
-Oh, yes.

245
00:16:56,140 --> 00:16:58,726
I can't catch a break today.

246
00:16:59,393 --> 00:17:01,687
We'll pack up your stuff,
meet you at the hospital.

247
00:17:01,896 --> 00:17:03,606
Sorry things turned out this way.

248
00:17:03,814 --> 00:17:09,570
Well, at this point, I'm just glad
someone's carrying me down the stairs.

249
00:17:13,866 --> 00:17:17,453
Met my childhood hero,
now I get to ride in an ambulance.

250
00:17:17,662 --> 00:17:21,290
Boy, if we can get him to do that calendar,
this will be the best day ever.

251
00:17:24,835 --> 00:17:26,504
I can't believe we lost her.

252
00:17:26,754 --> 00:17:29,549
What was I thinking? I'd be a terrible mom.

253
00:17:29,757 --> 00:17:31,217
Maybe with the first one.

254
00:17:31,425 --> 00:17:34,845
But kids are like pancakes,
the first one's always a throwaway.

255
00:17:39,183 --> 00:17:40,851
-How's this look?
-It's fine.

256
00:17:41,352 --> 00:17:46,190
-Where'd you get that picture of her?
-It's not her. I Googled "foo-foo little dogs."

257
00:17:46,399 --> 00:17:47,817
[SKYPE JINGLE PLAYING ON COMPUTER]

258
00:17:48,025 --> 00:17:50,945
It's Raj. Stay quiet.

259
00:17:52,029 --> 00:17:56,450
Hey, heh, bad timing. Bernadette just took
Cinnamon out for a walk.

260
00:17:57,118 --> 00:18:01,205
Interesting. Did they take a walk
down Liars' Lane?

261
00:18:02,373 --> 00:18:04,542
-What?
-A lane frequented by liars.

262
00:18:04,792 --> 00:18:07,378
Like you, you big liar.

263
00:18:08,588 --> 00:18:11,716
-You have her?
-Thank God she's okay.

264
00:18:11,924 --> 00:18:16,596
Well, I trusted you and you let me down.
The poor thing's been shaking for hours.

265
00:18:16,804 --> 00:18:19,640
-I'm really sorry.
-Hang on, you've had her for hours?

266
00:18:19,849 --> 00:18:23,686
Yes. I picked her up and then we both went
for massages to try and calm down.

267
00:18:23,894 --> 00:18:25,896
And then we got Pinkberry.

268
00:18:27,440 --> 00:18:30,735
You knew she was okay and you couldn't
pick up the phone to tell us?

269
00:18:31,319 --> 00:18:33,988
-Well, I thought about--
-Don't "well" me, mister.

270
00:18:34,196 --> 00:18:35,448
We've been worried sick.

271
00:18:35,656 --> 00:18:39,410
She could have been dead for all we knew.
You should be ashamed of yourself.

272
00:18:39,827 --> 00:18:41,912
-Sorry, I just--
-Sorry's not good enough.

273
00:18:42,120 --> 00:18:45,625
Maybe you need to take some time
and think about what you've done.

274
00:18:50,338 --> 00:18:54,800
Nice guilt trip. Heh.
You are gonna be an amazing mom.

275
00:18:55,009 --> 00:18:56,177
[BOTH CHUCKLE]

276
00:19:00,765 --> 00:19:03,768
[SINGING]
Soft kitty, warm kitty

277
00:19:04,018 --> 00:19:07,730
Little ball of fur

278
00:19:09,523 --> 00:19:12,443
Happy kitty, sleepy kitty

279
00:19:12,652 --> 00:19:15,613
Purr, purr, purr

280
00:19:15,946 --> 00:19:18,366
Thank you, Sheldon. That was very nice.

281
00:19:18,574 --> 00:19:20,868
-Want me to sing it again?
-No.

282
00:19:23,371 --> 00:19:26,957
The fourth time was the charm.

283
00:19:27,458 --> 00:19:32,338
There anything I can get for you?
Some apple juice? Uh, some Jell-O?

284
00:19:32,546 --> 00:19:37,259
No, no, thank you, but I do
have a favor to ask.

285
00:19:37,802 --> 00:19:40,471
-Name it.
-Well, I'm booked to do...

286
00:19:40,680 --> 00:19:43,015
...a children's party tomorrow...

287
00:19:43,224 --> 00:19:46,102
...and frankly, I don't feel up to it.

288
00:19:46,310 --> 00:19:49,939
Oh, you're not. You look awful.

289
00:19:52,400 --> 00:19:54,402
Thank you.

290
00:19:55,820 --> 00:20:00,950
Anyway, uh, you know
my act better than anybody...

291
00:20:01,158 --> 00:20:04,328
...I was hoping that
maybe you'd fill in for me.

292
00:20:05,871 --> 00:20:09,458
Are you saying that you want me
to be Professor Proton?

293
00:20:09,917 --> 00:20:12,837
-Yeah.
-Oh, my.

294
00:20:13,087 --> 00:20:15,965
What an honor.
Oh, this is like being asked to...

295
00:20:16,173 --> 00:20:19,844
...ascend Mount Olympus
and dine with the gods.

296
00:20:20,886 --> 00:20:25,683
Or a Korean family in Alhambra.

297
00:20:27,143 --> 00:20:29,520
But they'll know I'm not you.

298
00:20:29,729 --> 00:20:34,024
Should I call myself Professor Proton, Jr?

299
00:20:34,734 --> 00:20:36,026
Sounds great.

300
00:20:36,819 --> 00:20:41,991
So in a way, it's like I'm your son.

301
00:20:43,033 --> 00:20:45,035
Whatever.

302
00:20:48,205 --> 00:20:49,874
Father.

303
00:20:53,377 --> 00:20:55,629
Sure, what the hell.

